WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.362 --> 00:00:12.783
was a proud man who ruled the city of Corinth.

2
00:00:13.384 --> 00:00:15.145
But he is not remembered for his reign.

3
00:00:15.846 --> 00:00:21.050
Sisyphus is famous for another reason; he was one of the few people ever to return from the

4
00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:27.334
underworld. Brilliant and notorious, Sisyphus was known everywhere for his avarice and deceit.

5
00:00:27.875 --> 00:00:33.030
Sisyphus seduced his own niece, stole his brother's throne, and often killed his own guests!

6
00:00:33.830 --> 00:00:38.793
In the eyes of the gods, however, the biggest crime among all his terrible deeds was his betrayal

7
00:00:38.833 --> 00:00:39.313
of Zeus.

8
00:00:40.033 --> 00:00:44.276
Sisyphus was looking for a new source of water for his city when he saw an opportunity.

9
00:00:44.296 --> 00:00:50.259
Aegina, the daughter of Asopus, a river god, had secretly left home to be with her true love,

10
00:00:50.279 --> 00:00:56.302
Zeus. The couple soon started living together in the wooded valley close to Corinth, and they were often

11
00:00:56.382 --> 00:00:59.404
seen walking hand in hand near the outskirts of the city.

12
00:01:00.244 --> 00:01:04.425
When the angry river god came looking for his daughter, Sisyphus made a deal with him.

13
00:01:05.205 --> 00:01:10.447
He agreed to divulge Aegina's whereabouts in exchange for a new supply of water for his city.

14
00:01:11.327 --> 00:01:16.929
Asopus agreed to the proposal and immediately created a beautiful spring behind the temple of Aphrodite.

15
00:01:17.789 --> 00:01:23.871
After seeing this wonderful water source, Sisyphus kept his word and told Asopus that Zeus had fallen in

16
00:01:23.931 --> 00:01:25.771
love with Aegina and taken her away.

17
00:01:26.751 --> 00:01:30.449
This news enraged Asopus, for he had always been loyal to Zeus.

18
00:01:31.349 --> 00:01:35.331
Asopus quickly found Zeus and Aegina and tried to exact his revenge.

19
00:01:36.092 --> 00:01:41.514
In order to kiss Aegina more comfortably, Zeus had carelessly left his thunderbolts hanging in a tree.

20
00:01:42.475 --> 00:01:47.077
So when Asopus rushed at him with a club, the unarmed king of the gods could only run

21
00:01:47.137 --> 00:01:53.760
away. Fleeing through the forest, Zeus turned himself into a rock, fooling Asopus, who ran past him.

22
00:01:54.472 --> 00:01:59.153
This gave Zeus a chance to change back into his divine form and return to the tree.

23
00:01:59.834 --> 00:02:04.475
There, he retrieved his weapons and promptly flung a thunderbolt that struck Asopus in the leg.

24
00:02:04.975 --> 00:02:07.656
From that day on, Asopus walked with a limp.

25
00:02:08.216 --> 00:02:14.918
Furious that Sisyphus had betrayed him, Zeus ordered Thanatos, the god of death, to bring Sisyphus to the

26
00:02:14.978 --> 00:02:17.839
underworld, where he would be severely punished.

27
00:02:19.099 --> 00:02:22.740
Sisyphus, however, was able to scheme his way out of this fate.

28
00:02:23.356 --> 00:02:28.741
When Thanatos came to make the arrest, Sisyphus noticed that he had brought a pair of metal bracelets

29
00:02:28.821 --> 00:02:34.525
joined by a chain, a clever new device to restrain prisoners, invented by the skilled Hephaestus.

30
00:02:35.266 --> 00:02:38.168
Sisyphus had never seen bracelets like these before.

31
00:02:39.329 --> 00:02:45.094
Pretending to be honored by the presence of such a high-ranking god, Sisyphus craftily told him how much

32
00:02:45.134 --> 00:02:48.917
he admired the bracelets and asked Thanatos to show him how they worked.

33
00:02:49.918 --> 00:02:55.581
Thanatos demonstrated on himself, and as he was doing so, Sisyphus snapped the bracelets shut.

34
00:02:56.182 --> 00:02:59.263
Then he quickly fastened a collar around the helpless god's neck.

35
00:02:59.963 --> 00:03:05.826
As the god pleaded, yelled, and threatened him, Sisyphus mocked Thanatos' surprise and outrage.

36
00:03:06.406 --> 00:03:10.648
Triumphant, Sisyphus dragged the god home and locked him in a closet.

37
00:03:11.288 --> 00:03:14.689
The imprisonment of Thanatos caused chaos throughout the world.

38
00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:18.131
While he was away from his duties, no one could die.

39
00:03:19.071 --> 00:03:23.077
The old, the sick, and the wounded were forced to endure in pain and misery.

40
00:03:23.597 --> 00:03:27.438
People could not slaughter animals for meat, and they began to starve.

41
00:03:28.158 --> 00:03:34.200
All of the gods banded together against Sisyphus and threatened to make life so miserable for him that

42
00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:35.540
he would wish to die too.

43
00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:42.282
Still, they could not convince him to let Thanatos go; nor could they find the god to release

44
00:03:42.362 --> 00:03:49.064
him. After a month of this turmoil, the god of war, Ares, arrived at Sisyphus' home and finally

45
00:03:49.144 --> 00:03:50.344
liberated Thanatos.

46
00:03:51.388 --> 00:03:57.310
As soon as he was free, the god angrily decreed that Sisyphus would spend eternity suffering in the

47
00:03:57.370 --> 00:03:59.610
underworld, starting that very day.

48
00:04:00.751 --> 00:04:03.791
The devious Sisyphus, however, had other plans.

49
00:04:05.072 --> 00:04:10.893
Sisyphus told his wife not to bury his body, and then he left for the underworld, apparently ready

50
00:04:10.933 --> 00:04:11.914
to accept his fate.

51
00:04:12.954 --> 00:04:17.475
As soon as he arrived, he sought out Persephone, the queen of the underworld.

52
00:04:18.075 --> 00:04:23.590
He explained to her that his wife had humiliated him and denied him an honorable death by not

53
00:04:23.630 --> 00:04:26.652
burying him or offering the necessary sacrifices.

54
00:04:27.672 --> 00:04:32.636
Because she had not buried him with a coin under his tongue as was customary, he could not

55
00:04:32.696 --> 00:04:36.238
pay the ferryman, Charon, for passage across the River Styx.

56
00:04:36.959 --> 00:04:40.961
For these reasons, he claimed that he did not belong in the land of the dead.

57
00:04:41.822 --> 00:04:47.506
Sisyphus argued that he must be allowed to return to Corinth to avenge this disgrace and arrange for

58
00:04:47.546 --> 00:04:48.426
a proper burial.

59
00:04:49.387 --> 00:04:56.004
The kindhearted Persephone deliberated for a while and finally decided to let Sisyphus do as he asked.

60
00:04:56.565 --> 00:05:01.809
She stipulated that he must return the next day properly buried, with a coin under his tongue.

61
00:05:02.750 --> 00:05:10.036
Feigning sincerity, Sisyphus promised he would return, but as soon as he left the underworld, Sisyphus began to

62
00:05:10.076 --> 00:05:13.359
congratulate himself on regaining his life aboveground.

63
00:05:14.660 --> 00:05:20.632
Confident that he had escaped death, Sisyphus gathered his family and friends and bragged about how he had

64
00:05:20.712 --> 00:05:26.934
imprisoned Thanatos in a closet and tricked Persephone into letting him return to the world of the living.

65
00:05:28.655 --> 00:05:35.137
The cunning Sisyphus lived happily for many more years, but despite all his efforts, even he could not

66
00:05:35.197 --> 00:05:36.297
escape the inevitable.

67
00:05:37.217 --> 00:05:42.099
When Sisyphus finally died as an old man, he was dragged back to the underworld.

68
00:05:43.139 --> 00:05:48.533
There, he was met by the three judges of the dead, who assigned souls to their rightful places

69
00:05:48.593 --> 00:05:49.413
in the underworld.

70
00:05:50.293 --> 00:05:56.195
The judges did not look very kindly on lawbreakers, especially those who committed crimes against the gods.

71
00:05:56.935 --> 00:06:03.937
They decided to create a punishment that would fit Sisyphus' terrible trickery: Sisyphus would be condemned for all

72
00:06:04.017 --> 00:06:07.058
eternity to roll a huge boulder up a steep hill.

73
00:06:07.818 --> 00:06:09.278
It was a terrible punishment.

74
00:06:09.978 --> 00:06:15.900
The great boulder was very heavy, and pushing it only a few steps required great strength and stamina.

75
00:06:16.708 --> 00:06:22.612
Even worse, every time Sisyphus struggled up the hill, the rock slipped from his grasp just before he

76
00:06:22.672 --> 00:06:24.754
reached the top and rolled back down.

77
00:06:25.574 --> 00:06:30.197
Sisyphus was then compelled to return to the bottom of the hill and start pushing the rock up

78
00:06:30.217 --> 00:06:30.878
the hill again.

79
00:06:31.818 --> 00:06:37.862
This was Sisyphus' eternal fate—to push the rock up the hill over and over, only to watch it

80
00:06:37.902 --> 00:06:38.743
roll down again.

81
00:06:39.824 --> 00:06:45.607
Forced to suffer an eternity of toil and frustration, Sisyphus was finally punished by the gods for his

82
00:06:45.667 --> 00:06:46.188
arrogance.
